{
  "id": "zarzuela_cubana",
  "name": "Zarzuela cubana (Havana lyric theatre)",
  "family": "classical",
  "lineage": "Cuban branch of Spanish lyric theatre whose golden age came in Havana in the 1930s, with works staged from the late twenties into the forties, many of them first at the Teatro Martí, setting stories of colonial Cuba to scores that mix operatic arias and choruses with habanera, danza, guajira, son and Afro-Cuban numbers. Its best-known works are Gonzalo Roig's Cecilia Valdés (1932), Ernesto Lecuona's María la O (1930) and El cafetal, and Rodrigo Prats's Amalia Batista (1936). The voice card carries the soloists and chorus, and the string-quartet card stands in for the theatre orchestra's string section.",
  "recipe": "classic tropical mid-1930s zarzuela cubana, art Music, western common practice branch. slow rate, live take. string quartet inst, controlled legato flute, grenadilla African blackwood traditional legato clarinet, yellow brass canonical 70 30 bach stradivarius legato student friendly trumpet. live tracking. large-diaphragm condenser, tube pre, colored vintage console, vari-mu, Pultec passive program EQ. tape 15ips.",
